## Solution logic:

- arg1 = padding + address of puts@GOT
- arg2 = address of m


+ Set second->str to the GOT entry of puts.
+ Then use the second argument to write the address of m into that GOT entry.
+ When puts("~~") runs, it jumps to m, which prints the secret from g_buffer.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

[ s1 (id, str) ][ s2 (id, str) ][ buffer1 (8 bytes) ][ buffer2 (8 bytes) ]

The real bug is the first strcpy(s1->str, argv[1]): since s1->str only points to an 8‑byte heap buffer, any longer input spills into adjacent heap memory. In our case the next struct’s pointer s2->str. Later, the second strcpy(s2->str, argv[2]) uses that corrupted pointer, so it writes to an attacker‑chosen address.

First input overflows and overwrites second->str so it points to 0x8049928 (the puts@GOT entry).
Second input is written via strcpy(second->str, argv[2]), so it writes into that GOT slot, replacing the function pointer with 0x80484f4 (the address of m).
So after that, the value stored at 0x8049928 becomes 0x80484f4, and the next puts("~~") jumps to m().
